\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 Here's what you need to check:\par
1. Firewall Settings (Windows)\par
Windows has a built-in firewall that may block incoming or outgoing traffic on certain ports, including the port you're trying to use for your Kubernetes service (like 31661 or any custom NodePort). You need to allow traffic on that port.\par
\par
To Allow a Port Through the Windows Firewall:\par
Open Windows Firewall Settings:\par
\par
Press Windows Key + R to open the Run dialog.\par
Type wf.msc and press Enter to open Windows Firewall with Advanced Security.\par
Allow Port through Firewall:\par
\par
In the left pane, select Inbound Rules.\par
In the right pane, click New Rule.\par
Choose Port and click Next.\par
Select TCP and specify the port range (e.g., 31661 or a custom NodePort like 30000).\par
Click Next, choose Allow the connection, and click Next again.\par
Select the appropriate profiles (Domain, Private, or Public) and click Next.\par
Give the rule a name (e.g., Allow NodePort 31661) and click Finish.\par
Repeat for Outbound Rules (if necessary):\par
\par
If you have trouble accessing the service even after allowing inbound connections, repeat the steps for Outbound Rules.\par
\par

